<div class="forqan-page-ltr root-study-page" dir="ltr" lang="en">

  <section class="root-hero">
    <p class="root-kicker">Qur’anic Roots</p>
    <h1>The Qur’anic Root M L K</h1>
    <p class="root-subtitle">Ownership, kingship, authority, sovereignty, power of disposal, and the dependence of every ownership and dominion upon The God</p>
  </section>

  <section class="root-section" id="forms-covered">
    <h2>Forms Covered from This Root</h2>

    <p>
      In this page, the root <strong>م ل ک</strong> is examined semantically, then the most important Qur’anic forms from this root are explained separately:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>مَلَكَ / يَمْلِكُ</strong> — Form I verb; he owned, possessed, had power of disposal, owns, or has authority</li>
      <li><strong>مَالِك / مَٰلِك</strong> — active participle; owner, master, authority-holder, true possessor</li>
      <li><strong>مَلْك</strong> — noun; rule, kingship, or governing authority in a specific usage</li>
      <li><strong>مَلِك / مُلُوك</strong> — noun; king, ruler, kings</li>
      <li><strong>مَلِيك</strong> — noun/adjective; mighty sovereign, one possessing kingship and authority</li>
      <li><strong>مُلْك</strong> — noun/verbal noun; dominion, kingdom, sovereignty, kingship, broad ownership, or domain of authority</li>
      <li><strong>مَلَك / مَلَائِكَة</strong> — noun; angel / angels, beings commissioned within the system of divine command</li>
      <li><strong>مَلَكُوت</strong> — noun; malakūt, the hidden and deep domain of divine sovereignty and authority</li>
      <li><strong>مَمْلُوك</strong> — passive participle; one owned by another, a servant, or one who does not possess independent authority</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      Note: Some words such as <strong>مَكَانَة، مَكَّنَ، يُمَكِّنُ</strong> may be semantically close to power, establishment, and enabling, but in Qur’anic root classification they belong under the root <strong>م ك ن</strong>, not <strong>م ل ک</strong>. Therefore, they are not counted in this page as direct forms of the root <strong>م ل ک</strong>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Also, <strong>مِلْك</strong> exists as a known Arabic word, but in the Qur’anic forms of this root, the central Qur’anic form connected to dominion and broad ownership is <strong>مُلْك</strong>. Our focus is on the Qur’anic forms of the root <strong>م ل ک</strong>.
    </p>
  </section>

